00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:03.830 align:start position:0%
one of the most striking features of the  world around us is the remarkable  diversity  that is observed within communities  whether you're looking at  the community of trees in a rainforest  of  animals coexisting in the savannah or at  the microscale of microbial communities  coexisting in the soil my name is jeff  gore  i am an associate professor in the  physics department at mit the  massachusetts institute of technology  i am an experimental physicist  interested in trying to understand  the dynamics of complex communities of  biological organisms  and in particular laboratory microcosms  little worlds in the lab of  microorganisms interacting  and trying to understand what is it that  controls  the diversity of those communities  the coexistence of so many species in  natural communities  is a challenge to understand because we  often think about  some notion of the survival of the  fittest that  the species that is perhaps most adapted  to some environment  should perhaps be able to drive all the  other species there extinct  and if that's what's going on then when  you go out and you go on a walk  what you would observe is just a small  number of species in that environment  whereas in fact when you go on a hike  through the rainforest you see a  remarkable diversity of species that  seem to be able to  survive together and coexist  indefinitely  i think that my background in physics  has influenced me in a number of  different ways in terms of how i think  about these problems  of ecosystem dynamics and the diversity  of communities  but perhaps the most important way in  which it's influenced my work  is that in physics there's a tradition  of a very strong  dialogue between theory and experiment  where  theory often guides which measurements  or experiments  one does but then also the experiments  will  guide future theories this complex  interplay between theory and experiment  is i think a really fun way of doing  science but also a way of doing science  that provides a lot of insight  into what's going on as we try to  learn about the rules governing say  microbial community diversity but  there's always a question about the  relationship between  what we discover as compared to the  dynamics of communities at the macra  scale  between treaties or animals and i from  my perspective  i think that some of the lessons that we  learn for microbial communities  will actually have general implications  for  other forms of communities but at the  same time it's likely they're going to  be some differences  and actually both the similarities and  the differences are things that we're  excited to try to understand
 

